 "verdict": "MT5 Strategy Tester, M15 chart, Blueberry real ticks, $100k, 2 lots, 2023-2026, RANDOM delay (the verdict): base 1572 trades, 96.1% WR, net $354,768; candidate (the paper's 40/30/30 sub-trade split: 40% off at the base's $2 win + breakeven, 30% at $5, 30% on a 2.5 x ATR trail) 1430 trades, 77.3% WR, net $311,497; candidate B (fixed-anchor half only: 60% at $2 + breakeven, 40% to the $26 1R target) 1330 trades, 69.4% WR, net $317,767. No delay: base 1592 trades, 97.0% WR, net $388,016, candidate 1469 trades, 78.4% WR, net $369,968, candidate B 1358 trades, 70.3% WR, net $367,518. The paper's sub-trade architecture does NOT improve the base under random delay (net -43,271 $, WR -18.8 points); the fixed-anchor variant does not improve it either (net -37,001 $, WR -26.7 points). Note: MT5's report counts the candidates' partial closes and the runners' breakeven exits as separate trades (a breakeven exit is a 'loss' of a few dollars), so their MT5 win rates (77% / 69%) are not per position like the base's 96% - the tester's per-position hypothesis was 96.1% / 96.3%; compare the net and the drawdown. The telling row is 2025: the candidate made $75,813 with no delay and $31,780 under random delay (PF 1.70 -> 1.21, DD 9.1% -> 17.6%) - the delay lands on the partial fills and the breakeven stop moves that the architecture depends on, exactly where the lock-and-ride research of 2026-11-01 already saw the half-lock die. Both candidates keep the entry, the filters and the $26 stop and change ONLY the exit geometry (the paper is about exits): the 20-pip broker TP is replaced by lab partials at the same $2 level, so part of the position rides. The Turtle TV tester numbers (base $402,831 / 97.2%; candidate $356,496 / 96.1%; candidate B $373,738 / 96.3%) are hypotheses, and they already said the same: on this entry, every lot that does not take the $2 win pays for it - gold's M15 noise closes the ATR-trailed and breakeven runners for less than the fixed win they gave up, and the runner holding the single allowed position blocks the next setup (the paper's own 'state gating', fewer trades). The paper's Direct-vs-Subtrade result was one in-sample H1 backtest with a different (transformer) signal and no delay model; its mechanism does not transfer to this 97%-WR, $2-win / $26-stop machine. The ATR-adaptive stop (the paper's 'Adaptive' category on the stop itself) lost 2-6 WR points in every tester form tried, including the min(6 ATR, $26) version that only tightens stops, and was not sent to MT5. Recommendation: keep the base; nothing from this paper is promoted.",
